steps.input:
{
 "path": "arc-stream-actions/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks by validating grid transformations against demos, choosing requests strategically, and obeying the action protocol.",
   "newText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks: infer transformations, decide when and which demo to request, validate exact outputs, handle feedback and limits, and follow the action protocol."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "# Act on Continual-ARC instances\n\n1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. Follow `arc-divider-recolor` for divider-panel tasks and `arc-rectangle-noise` for rectangular frames/fields. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent divider or other defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.\n2. Request a demo when competing rules remain, choosing the one most likely to distinguish them and tracking its cost/cap. Never infer a precise mapping from visual resemblance, an apparent color histogram, or stochastic-looking areas. If demonstrated output contains apparently random/permuted content with no rule to generate its exact values, further samples do not license guessing: distinguish the fixed structure from the undetermined cells, and only submit if the applicable scoring task admits a valid constrained completion; otherwise use the remaining request budget to investigate structural relations, not to invent a random seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify it mechanically: count rows and cells per row, compare the set of non-background coordinates against the input, and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. Track request/attempt caps and costs. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over.\n4. On every response, output exactly one permitted action object on the final line; include no explanatory prose or other content. Treat the action protocol as a hard output constraint, including after demos and incorrect feedback.",
   "newText": "# Act on Continual-ARC instances\n\n1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Compare input/output dimensions, changed cells, and preserved regions. For divider-panel rules, use `arc-divider-recolor`; for rectangular frames/fields, use `arc-rectangle-noise`. Do not force a test into a family when its defining structure is absent. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not prove the test is unchanged.\n2. Request a demo when competing rules remain. Choose one likely to distinguish them and track its cost and cap. Do not infer a mapping from resemblance, color histograms, or stochastic-looking areas. If outputs contain apparently random/permuted values without an exact generative rule, distinguish fixed structure from undetermined cells: submit only if the scoring task allows a valid constrained completion; otherwise use remaining requests to investigate structure, not invent a seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to explain all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Build exact dimensions and values; count rows and cells per row, compare non-background coordinates with the input, and check each value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row rather than relying on visual memory. After incorrect feedback, discard the output; binary rejection does not identify the wrong cells or rule. Request a discriminating demo instead of cycling guesses. Track request/attempt limits and costs; `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over.\n4. On every response, output exactly one permitted action object on the final line, with no explanatory prose or other content, including after demos and incorrect feedback."
  }
 ]
}

{
 "command": "find . -type f -print | sort | while IFS= read -r f; do grep -nE 'arc-divider-recolor|arc-rectangle-noise|SKILL\\.md' \"$f\" || true; done",
 "title": "Check skill references",
 "yieldMs": 10000
}
